|~ qlang operand catalog — codeAsData family. The ring that
   closes source-text → AST-Map → pipeValue without leaving the
   language. `parse` lifts a String (or a Quote-value) into an
   AST-Map shape documented in `core/src/ast-codec.mjs`;
   `apply(code)` runs the Quote-or-Map its captured arg answers
   against the subject, subject first like every other operand.
   Trail-emitted suffix Quotes flow through pipeValue, so
   `error !| /trail | as(:t) | start | apply(t)` re-runs deflected
   steps against a fresh subject. ~|

:parse
  |~~ Parses a source string into an AST-Map — the data-form
      representation of the program described by the
      ast-codec.mjs astNodeToMap codec. Half of the codeAsData
      ring; pair
      with apply(/) to round-trip between source text and
      evaluated pipeValue without leaving qlang. Parse errors
      from the underlying peggy parser lift onto the fail-track
      as ordinary error values, so malformed sources surface
      through !| like every other runtime failure.

      ~{"42" | parse | /:kind | eq(:NumberLit)}
      ~{"add(1, 2)" | parse | /name | eq("add")}
  ~~|
  ::builtin{:impl :qlang/prim/parse
            :category :codeAsData
            :subject :string
            :returns :map}

::ParseSubjectNotStringOrQuoteError
  |~~ `parse` requires a String or Quote subject.

      ~{
        42 | parse
        !| [type /actualType]
        |  eq([::ParseSubjectNotStringOrQuoteError :number])
       }
  ~~|

:apply
  |~~ Runs the code its captured arg answers — a Quote, or an
      AST-Map from parse — against the subject, under the fork
      rule: the declarations the code makes stay inside it, and
      only its value comes out. The subject comes first, as for
      every other operand, so `x | apply(/)` runs a quote held
      as the subject against itself, and `"src" | parse |
      apply(/)` evaluates source text.

      The Quote's first step rides `|` against the subject
      unless the Quote carries a leading combinator (`*`, `!|`),
      which routes it through that combinator. A pipeline-suffix
      shape — exactly the form `:trail` records surface — is
      therefore directly re-executable.

      ~{42 | apply(~{type | eq(:null)}) | eq(false)}
      ~{[1 2 3] | apply(~{* mul(2)}) | eq([2 4 6])}
      ~{[[1 2] [3 4]] | apply(~{flat | sort}) | eq([1 2 3 4])}
      ~{"10 | add(3)" | parse | apply(/) | eq(13)}
  ~~|
  ::builtin{:impl :qlang/prim/apply
            :category :codeAsData
            :subject :any
            :returns :any
            :modifiers [[:quote :map]]}


::ApplyCodeNotMapOrQuoteError
  |~~ `apply` requires its captured arg to answer a Map (AST-Map)
      or a Quote (source-form) — the code it runs against the
      subject.

      ~{
        42 | apply(1)
        !| [type /actualType]
        |  eq([::ApplyCodeNotMapOrQuoteError :number])
       }
  ~~|
